synthesized samples were separated by centrifugation and further digested for ICP measurements. The results suggest that absorbed amounts of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ measured by ICP were wellmatched with the UV data (Table S3 and Figure S21).
Given the high adsorption capacity of PC $V{-}134{-}22\%\mathrm{TC}$ PP, selective adsorption tests were performed for a series of anions. As shown in Figure S22, the adsorption capacity of PCN$134{\cdot}22\%\mathrm{TCPP}$ was maintained even when 2-fold excess moles of disturbing ions $\mathrm{F^{-}}$ , Cl–, $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ , ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}^{-}$ , and $\mathrm{SO_{4}}^{2-}$ ) were added, compared with the $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$ adsorption capacity in the absence of disturbing ions. Meanwhile, the XRPD pattern indicates that the network of the best performing material, $\mathrm{PCN}{-}134{-}22\%\mathrm{TCPP}$ , was maintained after $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ adsorption (Figure S23).
Moreover, the photocatalytic reduction of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ to $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})$ was also conducted in aqueous solution with a $\mathfrak{p H}$ value close to 6.28 After being stirred for $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ to reach an adsorptiondesorption equilibrium in a dark environment, the suspensions were irradiated by a 300 W Xe lamp with $420\ \mathrm{nm}$ cut-off filter. The dichromate concentration was calculated by comparing the UV/vis absorbance (at $\lambda=257~\mathrm{{nm}}$ ). As shown in Figure 6b and Table S4, PCN-134 with $22\%$ TCPP molar ratio exhibited the highest photocatalytic activity, which is higher than wellknown photocatalysts such as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ . It’s worth mentioning that the performance of PCN-134 exceeded that of MOF-525, which is also composed of a TCPP linker and a 12-connected $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Zr}}_{6}$ cluster, suggesting the positive contribution of defect sites to the catalytic activities. We inferred that the binding of dichromate on the defect sites of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Zr}_{6}}$ clusters facilitates the electron transfer process from photosensitizer to substrates and thus the reduction from $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})$ to $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})$ Based on the experimental data, a tentative mechanism proposed for the photoreduction of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ by PCN-134 is described in Figure S24. The porphyrin moieties from the linker were excited by visible light to inject electrons into $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Zr}_{6}}$ clusters, yielding $\left[\mathrm{\bar{TCPP}}\right]^{+}$ . The binding dichromates on $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Zr}_{6}}$ clusters were then reduced by the photoelectron into $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})$ , and the $\left[\mathrm{TCPP}\right]^{+}$ was reduced back to TCPP by ethanol. Additionally, the subsequent XRPD pattern suggests that the framework of PCN-134 was still retained after $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ reduction (Figure S25).
## CONCLUSION
In conclusion, we have developed a thermodynamically guided strategy for the synthesis of two mixed-linker $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs: PCN-133 and PCN-134. Their 3D layer-pillar structures were
## Journal of the American Chemical Society
built from a 2D kdg layer, which consists of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Zr}_{6}}$ clusters and BTBs as the primary linkers, supported by auxiliary DCDPS or TCPP linkers. PCN-134 exhibits high porosity and excellent chemical stability in aqueous solutions with pH scale ranges from 0 to 13. More importantly, the mixed-linker $Z\mathbf{r}.$ - MOF, PCN-134, has significant tolerance of partial auxiliarylinker absence during the assembly process, leading to a high density of structural defects while preserving the overall structure. In addition, the defect density can be systematically tuned by adjusting the linker ratios, which in turn alters the properties of PCN-134. For instance, $\mathrm{PCN-}134{\mathrm{-}}25\%\mathrm{TCPP}$ exhibits the highest nitrogen uptake and P $\mathrm{CN}{-}134{-}22\%\mathrm{T}$ CPP exhibits the highest $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$ absorbance compared with those of other TCPP molar ratios in PCN-134 system. Furthermore, $\mathrm{PCN-}134{\mathrm{-}}22\%\mathrm{TCPP}$ exhibits excellent photocatalytic activity toward the reduction of dichromate in aqueous solution under visible light irradiation. Apart from providing two new mixedlinker $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs, the strategy developed herein shall lead to a facile synthesis of a stable $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF with a controllable defect density, which should extend the application of MOF materials in the near future.
